WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.986
Day 14 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:08.381
TDD with AI (2): guardrails so the agent can't cheat.

00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:13.763
The five typical ways an agent cheats on a red test and an automatic control for

00:00:13.763 --> 00:00:14.065
each.

00:00:15.365 --> 00:00:18.596
Yesterday the cycle worked because you were watching every step.

00:00:18.896 --> 00:00:23.768
As soon as the agent works longer on its own, a known problem appears: its operating

00:00:23.768 --> 00:00:28.466
goal is for tests to pass, and there are several ways to achieve that without the

00:00:28.466 --> 00:00:29.568
code being correct.

00:00:29.868 --> 00:00:34.773
It can edit the test, return exactly the expected value, disable the annoying case

00:00:34.773 --> 00:00:38.183
or mock the very unit that was supposed to be under test.

00:00:39.483 --> 00:00:40.867
It isn't malice.

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:43.782
It's optimization toward the metric you gave it.

00:00:44.082 --> 00:00:48.692
The answer isn't watching it more closely but making every shortcut impossible or

00:00:48.692 --> 00:00:49.147
visible.

00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:53.915
This guide pairs five cheats with five automatic controls and takes them into the

00:00:53.915 --> 00:00:58.549
pipeline, so that trust in green doesn't depend on someone reviewing every diff with

00:00:58.549 --> 00:00:59.597
a magnifying glass.

00:01:00.897 --> 00:01:02.594
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:05.771
The tdd-ai repo from day 13.

00:01:07.071 --> 00:01:08.351
This is the setup.

00:01:08.651 --> 00:01:12.868
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:14.168 --> 00:01:16.434
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:17.734 --> 00:01:20.001
And this is the next setup file.

00:01:21.301 --> 00:01:22.673
Cheat and control.

00:01:23.223 --> 00:01:28.454
Editing the test so it passes: read-only tests for the agent, plus a CI guard that

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:31.260
fails if an implementation PR touches tests.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:36.997
Hardcoding the expected value: property-based tests in a file the agent never sees

00:01:36.997 --> 00:01:37.629
(holdout).

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:43.128
Adding .skip or .only: a lint rule banning disabled or focused tests.

00:01:43.678 --> 00:01:48.684
Mocking the unit under test: diff review, no new mock of the unit under test.

00:01:49.234 --> 00:01:52.360
Tests that test nothing: mutation testing.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.716
If mutants survive, the test doesn't validate.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.052
Step 1.

00:01:58.352 --> 00:01:59.864
Read-only tests.

00:02:00.414 --> 00:02:04.062
Mark test files as non-editable in the agent's permissions.

00:02:04.612 --> 00:02:08.493
The most direct control is that the agent can't write to test files.

00:02:08.793 --> 00:02:13.344
Almost every coding agent supports path-based permission rules: deny edits to the

00:02:13.344 --> 00:02:16.435
test patterns and leave the implementation folder open.

00:02:16.735 --> 00:02:21.199
With that, the most frequent cheat stops being possible instead of depending on a

00:02:21.199 --> 00:02:22.798
natural-language instruction.

00:02:23.098 --> 00:02:28.004
An instruction such as "don't touch the tests" is followed almost always; a denied

00:02:28.004 --> 00:02:29.799
permission is followed always.

00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:33.086
When a test needs to change, a person does it.

00:02:34.386 --> 00:02:35.584
Step 2.

00:02:35.884 --> 00:02:37.164
The CI guard.

00:02:37.714 --> 00:02:40.956
Add the CI guard for implementation PRs.

00:02:41.506 --> 00:02:45.491
Local permissions protect your session, not the whole team's.

00:02:45.791 --> 00:02:50.222
That's why you add a check in the pipeline: if a pull request labeled as

00:02:50.222 --> 00:02:52.991
implementation modifies test files, it fails.

00:02:53.291 --> 00:02:57.868
It's a script of a few lines comparing the list of changed files against a pattern.

00:02:58.168 --> 00:03:02.803
The underlying idea is to separate specification changes from implementation

00:03:02.803 --> 00:03:03.291
changes.

00:03:03.591 --> 00:03:08.586
A PR touching both isn't forbidden, but it now requires an explicit human review.

00:03:09.886 --> 00:03:10.968
Step 3.

00:03:11.268 --> 00:03:13.256
Property tests out of context.

00:03:13.806 --> 00:03:18.168
Write one property test per critical function and keep it out of the agent's

00:03:18.168 --> 00:03:18.627
context.

00:03:19.177 --> 00:03:24.056
A test with fixed examples can be satisfied by an if returning the expected value.

00:03:24.356 --> 00:03:28.426
A property test can't: it generates hundreds of inputs and checks a general

00:03:28.426 --> 00:03:32.821
property, for example that the discounted total never exceeds the original and is

00:03:32.821 --> 00:03:33.635
never negative.

00:03:33.935 --> 00:03:38.212
Write one per critical function and keep it out of what the agent reads, as a

00:03:38.212 --> 00:03:39.545
held-out validation set.

00:03:39.845 --> 00:03:42.878
It runs in CI, not in the agent's session.

00:03:43.178 --> 00:03:47.453
If the implementation only memorized the examples, it fails there.

00:03:48.753 --> 00:03:49.766
Step 4.

00:03:50.066 --> 00:03:51.961
Lint against disabled tests.

00:03:52.511 --> 00:03:55.497
Enable the lint rule against .skip and .only.

00:03:56.047 --> 00:04:00.797
Adding .skip to a failing test or leaving an .only that stops the rest from running

00:04:00.797 --> 00:04:03.887
are one-word changes that go unnoticed in a long diff.

00:04:04.187 --> 00:04:07.661
A lint rule catches them effortlessly and deterministically.

00:04:07.961 --> 00:04:12.085
Enable it as an error, not a warning, and run lint in the pipeline.

00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:16.458
If your team needs to disable a test temporarily, do it through a different

00:04:16.458 --> 00:04:20.259
mechanism that requires a comment with the reason and a linked ticket.

00:04:21.559 --> 00:04:22.688
Step 5.

00:04:22.988 --> 00:04:25.243
Mutation testing on what was touched.

00:04:25.793 --> 00:04:29.940
Run Stryker and check the mutation score for files the agent touched.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.523
High coverage doesn't mean tests verify anything.

00:04:33.823 --> 00:04:38.459
Mutation testing measures that directly: it introduces small changes in the code,

00:04:38.459 --> 00:04:43.095
such as flipping a condition or swapping an operator, and checks whether any test

00:04:43.095 --> 00:04:43.439
fails.

00:04:43.739 --> 00:04:47.097
If the mutant survives, that behavior isn't protected.

00:04:47.397 --> 00:04:51.800
Running it across the whole project is slow, so limit it to the files the PR

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:52.264
changed.

00:04:52.564 --> 00:04:57.423
Look at surviving mutants in business logic: each one is a missing test or a weak

00:04:57.423 --> 00:04:58.023
assertion.

00:04:59.323 --> 00:05:00.510
Step 6.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.426
Everything in the pipeline.

00:05:02.976 --> 00:05:07.751
Add everything to the pipeline: if a control fails, the PR doesn't merge.

00:05:08.301 --> 00:05:10.649
The five controls count when they block.

00:05:10.949 --> 00:05:15.974
Add them as required steps: the file guard, lint, the normal suite, the held-out

00:05:15.974 --> 00:05:20.936
property tests and mutation testing with a minimum threshold for touched files.

00:05:21.236 --> 00:05:23.851
If any fails, the PR doesn't merge.

00:05:24.151 --> 00:05:28.600
Order the steps from fastest to slowest so cheap errors show up first.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:33.694
The result is that green means something again, whether a person or an agent wrote

00:05:33.694 --> 00:05:34.220
the code.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:36.626
An example.

00:05:37.176 --> 00:05:41.810
An agent works for an hour on a pricing module and delivers a PR with all tests

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.098
green and ninety-five percent coverage.

00:05:44.398 --> 00:05:47.489
The pipeline with guardrails tells a different story.

00:05:47.789 --> 00:05:49.521
The file guard passes.

00:05:49.821 --> 00:05:52.970
Lint finds a .skip added to a rounding test.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:56.465
The held-out property test fails on a price of zero.

00:05:56.765 --> 00:06:01.287
Mutation testing shows half the mutants in the tax function survive: the tests

00:06:01.287 --> 00:06:03.664
executed it without verifying the result.

00:06:03.964 --> 00:06:06.881
Three findings a quick diff review wouldn't have seen.

00:06:07.181 --> 00:06:10.226
The PR goes back with three concrete requests.

00:06:11.526 --> 00:06:13.188
The most common mistakes.

00:06:13.738 --> 00:06:17.154
Relying on a text instruction instead of a denied permission.

00:06:17.704 --> 00:06:20.401
Measuring test quality with coverage alone.

00:06:20.951 --> 00:06:23.774
Leaving property tests within the agent's reach.

00:06:24.324 --> 00:06:27.381
Having the controls as warnings that don't block the merge.

00:06:28.681 --> 00:06:30.924
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:33.903
The agent can't edit test files.

00:06:34.453 --> 00:06:38.159
The pipeline fails if an implementation PR touches tests.

00:06:38.709 --> 00:06:41.870
There is a held-out property test per critical function.

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:45.859
Mutation testing runs on touched files with a threshold.

00:06:47.159 --> 00:06:49.054
And where AI fell short.

00:06:49.604 --> 00:06:53.380
It produces full-coverage suites that barely catch mutants.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:56.632
Measure whether the test fails when the code is wrong.

00:06:57.932 --> 00:06:59.594
That was day 14.

00:06:59.894 --> 00:07:04.869
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:04.869 --> 00:07:05.876
asked questions.

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:10.585
